{
  "dom_id": "question-134228",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Of the $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of adult dog food that Leonard bought, he gave his dog Rocky $\\frac{1}{4}$ of a kilogram. As a mixed number, Leonard has\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{kg}$ of adult dog food remaining.",
  "worked_solution": "To find out how much adult dog food is remaining, we must calculate the difference $4-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nTo subtract $\\frac{1}{4}$ from $4,$ we need to express the whole number $4$ as an equivalent fraction with a denominator of $4.$\n\nFirst, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nThen, we put this fraction over a denominator of $4$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\times 4}{1\\times 4}=\\frac{16}{4}\n$$\n\nSo now, we have to find:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with like denominators, we subtract the numerators and keep the denominators the same. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{16}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{16-1}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15÷4=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Leonard had $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{kg}$ of adult dog food remaining.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
